></name><name><surname>Schulz</surname><given-names>TG</given-names></name><name><surname>Bunger</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Muller</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Hallier</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Thimerosal induces micronuclei in the cytochalasin B block micronucleus test with human lymphocytes</article-title><source>Arch Toxicol</source><year>2003</year><volume>77</volume><fpage>50</fpage><lpage>55</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'Arch Toxicol||77|50||bib55|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12491041</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib56"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wojewodzka</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Kruszewski</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Szumiel</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effect of signal transduction inhibition in adapted lymphocytes: micronuclei frequency and DNA repair</article-title><source>Int J Radiat Biol</source><year>1997</year><volume>71</volume><fpage>245</fpage><lpage>252</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'Int J Radiat Biol||71|245||bib56|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9134013</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Distribution of radiation-induced mean tail moments in the entire study population. Shaded boxes represent women who are <italic>BRCA1</italic><sup><italic>WT</italic></sup> and the black boxes represent women who are <italic>BRCA1</italic><sup><italic>&#x0002b;/&#x02212;</italic></sup>. The solid vertical line represents the cutoff point between radiosensitive and non-sensitive individuals and was based on the 90<sup>th</sup> percentile of the non-carrier population after 2&#x02009;Gy of <italic>&#x003b3;</italic>-irradiation and one hour of recovery time (cutoff=6.39).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6603528f1"/></fig><fig id="fig2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Distribution of radiation-induced micronucleus frequencies in the entire study population. Shaded boxes represent women who are <italic>BRCA1</italic><sup><italic>WT</italic></sup> and the black boxes represent women who are <italic>BRCA1</italic><sup><italic>&#x0002b;/&#x02212;</italic></sup>. The solid vertical line represents the cutoff point between radiosensitive and non-sensitive individuals and was based on the 90<sup>th</sup> percentile of the non-carrier population after 2&#x02009;Gy of <italic>&#x003b3;</italic>-irradiation and no recovery time (cutoff=197.69).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6603528f2"/></fig><fig id="fig3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Distribution of radiation-induced <italic>&#x003b3;</italic>-H2AX foci in the entire study population. Shaded boxes represent women who are <italic>BRCA1</italic><sup><italic>WT</italic></sup> and the black boxes represent women who are <italic>BRCA1</italic><sup><italic>&#x0002b;/&#x02212;</italic></sup>. The solid vertical line represents the cutoff point between radiosensitive and non-sensitive individuals and was based on the 90th percentile of the non-carrier population after 2&#x02009;Gy of <italic>&#x003b3;</italic>-irradiation and 3&#x02009;h of recovery time (cutoff=62.01).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6603528f3"/></fig><table-wrap id="tbl1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p content-type="table-title">Principal characteristics of the study participants, by mutation status</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1" width="85%"><colgroup><col align="left"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/></colgroup><thead valign="bottom"><tr><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50"